meta {
  name: WHEP endpoint
  type: http
  seq: 10
}

post {
  url: {{webrtcOrigin}}/{{groupPrefix}}{{streamName}}/whep
  body: text
  auth: none
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/sdp
  Authorization: Bearer {{relayAccessToken}}
}

body:text {
  v=0
}

tests {
  test("the WebRTC listener answers, and not the HLS one", function () {
    expect(res.getBody()).to.be.an("object");
    expect(res.getBody().error).to.contain("SDP");
  });
}

docs {
  # WHEP endpoint

  A routing check, not a session: the offer is one line of nothing, and what is read is who refuses it.

  The relay answers JSON, so a refusal naming SDP means the request reached the WebRTC listener with its credential intact.
  A plain-text `404 page not found` means it reached the HLS listener instead, which carries no such route, and that is what a reverse proxy matching `/whep` as a prefix does to every real endpoint: the address is the stream's own path with the verb after it, so nothing starts with `/whep` (`deploy/Caddyfile`).

  A real exchange is an SDP offer, an answer, and ICE candidates PATCHed to the resource the answer's `Location` names.
  No collection runs one, and the app opens it with `whepsrc` instead.
}
